#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1260.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184985} :activity/id #uuid "74cb5e70-9a9a-5b7d-b148-f583f043d560" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048506 :topic/id #uuid "a5765f97-9627-5a4e-ab27-f0369b4784bd" :topic/math-academy-id 3818} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184942 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184175 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184074 :question/id #uuid "edd4d491-c483-5004-b30f-68cf0ef76f43" :question/math-academy-id "e-5922"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d702975-166e-5067-ae3c-1152fade8248" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049970}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416358}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace"} :step/id #uuid "b3f00d8e-e0e0-5507-a417-bc0bae247d7f" :step/math-academy-id 21956 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184987}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184943 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184176 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184075 :question/id #uuid "04cb9ecc-68fa-546e-aa96-8a1ee050bbed" :question/math-academy-id "e-5923"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26b41192-9680-55c3-930c-52ff235e1800" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048445}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416357}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto the Solution Space of a Linear System"} :step/id #uuid "1b0aff21-5c4b-587f-a636-9265b1edc074" :step/math-academy-id 21957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184985 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185333 :tutorial/content "One use for finding the orthogonal projection of a vector onto a subspace is to find the shortest distance between the vector and the subspace.\n\nGiven a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and a subspace $S = \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ the **distance between $\\mathbf{x}$ and $S$** is given by \n\n$$\n\\Vert \\mathbf{x} - \\textrm{proj}_{S}\\mathbf{x} \\Vert\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/d1/d10fd1778529183deb6912cebe9d2b7ad405091fe21a261c464d3d22c6d0860b.png)\n\nLet's find the distance between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ where\n\n![](images/83/83f52159171e736c7ee9edbd0593442d2282572db667eca483e5a77d0b73c9f7.svg)\n\nThe orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S} \\: \\mathbf{x} = A(A^T\\!A)^{-1}\\!A^T\\,\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have \n![](images/9e/9eca38b4b340e44108b073e3f939ce29ae96345b9c1870b9e2cc57a98f9cb077.svg)\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 9\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the distance formula, the distance between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0-(-2))^{2}+(2-2)^{2}+(4-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+0+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** Recall that the vector $\\textbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x},$ whose norm $\\| \\textbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} \\|$ represents the distance from $\\mathbf{x}$ to $S,$ is sometimes called the **vector rejection of $\\mathbf{x}$ from $S.$**" :tutorial/id #uuid "97ecd7fd-ac4d-5cca-86c7-db064dd69609" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6131 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "290e4663-8f35-58a4-8aae-e48323b5562f" :step/math-academy-id 37246 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184942}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184986 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184207 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184111 :question/id #uuid "98aed663-0f72-5494-9a5f-ba2fdf94764a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9906"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1224e2dd-96bc-5c24-aacf-3c36a7c3a1e1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049045}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416359}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acute Angle Between a Vector and a Subspace"} :step/id #uuid "176d8f71-b7e6-5f7f-ace6-c9db9b253e57" :step/math-academy-id 37247 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184943}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184987 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185334 :tutorial/content "As well as finding the distance between a vector and a subspace, we can also use the vector's orthogonal projection onto the subspace to find the angle between the vector and the subspace.\n\nGiven a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and a subspace $S = \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ the **acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and $S$** is the acute angle formed by $\\mathbf{x}$ and its orthogonal projection $\\textrm{proj}_S \\mathbf{x},$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/1c/1c9fd00ab46691fefc65ffa6478ed589fbad1df6bfd19b913c6a4a94ae0358a0.png)\n\nLet's return to our previous example, where we had\n\n![](images/3c/3c881ed55614722cf3cfe5c969c8c05a0c093e87cf1d2aa1221dbfc741455b92.svg)\n\nPreviously, we found that the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ into the subspace spanned by $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$ is\n\n![](images/6a/6a060f96f72ece67567be5d0deb70042fa50ba2f005a1539c67383f6de3e1426.svg)\n\nWe can compute the cosine of the angle between $\\mathbf x$ and $\\textrm{proj}_S \\mathbf x$ using the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}x}{||x||\\cdot ||{\\text{proj}}_{S}x||} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{20}\\sqrt{12}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3}{2\\sqrt{5}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos\\left(  \\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{\\sqrt5}\\right) \\approx 39.23^{\\circ},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." :tutorial/id #uuid "4c19ec4b-3bdc-50e9-aac0-193e2ab2c298" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6132 :tutorial/title "Angle Between a Vector and a Subspace"} :step/id #uuid "53d452ce-ad18-557e-8201-0b1f1d4b9e5a" :step/math-academy-id 37248 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184986}}] :activity/title "Projecting Vectors Onto Subspaces in Euclidean Spaces (Arbitrary Bases): Applications" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186184403}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416362} :activity/id #uuid "c9eb477d-ec3c-5256-a223-de788147d2e5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048506 :topic/id #uuid "a5765f97-9627-5a4e-ab27-f0369b4784bd" :topic/math-academy-id 3818} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416360 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416358 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29430249-c511-5b3f-b606-e04e258a7382" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416328} {:answer/id #uuid "66516586-cecd-5e63-b1f0-d497a546cd06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416334} {:answer/id #uuid "b038fe34-a16a-5a55-bf98-fc3b8439343d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416342} {:answer/id #uuid "d9c0d55a-c5e9-50c2-8df7-9355a909c1d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416346} {:answer/id #uuid "f15ad28f-93e2-5d8b-b5d3-7600366bd867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416347}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416328} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b507b6b-07ae-51ff-8ef0-2463cc379cda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d93f91c3-07ee-5843-9236-38543eb4a92a" :question/math-academy-id "q-139474" :question/problem "Find the distance between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}6 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{11}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2-0)^{2}+(6-0)^{2}+(2-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+36+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{44} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "22bcf45c-640c-575f-a16d-c7b5c8b62a7e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416364}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416361 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184778 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09f25da8-1536-541d-983b-95cee16bd959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416326} {:answer/id #uuid "4e40efb6-25f0-52a4-b9bd-0bfe176c7b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416331} {:answer/id #uuid "8b4af8f4-7d15-5294-891c-2810e7b0aee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416337} {:answer/id #uuid "a6a69985-146d-572c-8484-4f24cfb9840f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 5 \\\\ \\frac{9}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416341} {:answer/id #uuid "be33eccb-cb9a-5eb3-8bb8-2724f8b921de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416344}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416337} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bd4b417-69d9-54a6-aef3-a9bc3d8656f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "857ad938-6f3a-5185-b8e2-e07fd7263904" :question/math-academy-id "q-40213" :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}],\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-9{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis for the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -9 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6a574829-aeac-59dc-b7f2-f0730e2342d5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416363}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416362 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416359 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fef03fc-79ed-52dc-bd31-1e9cf734dd91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416329} {:answer/id #uuid "5170028a-ab43-580a-a8b5-29da72f1fcd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416333} {:answer/id #uuid "7c4a4aea-dd6d-5e0a-99e0-02a535cf0fc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416336} {:answer/id #uuid "c52aa776-edc2-57f3-a183-a9201feae1aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416345} {:answer/id #uuid "f37da0e2-81e8-576a-aa5e-b3594802c8da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416348}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416333} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d5f218f-38ba-531a-af9e-df5b58a66d01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416352}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f6dcfad9-4fa8-567a-ab9a-f61e9f6b1a76" :question/math-academy-id "q-39856" :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$  \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}x}{‖x‖‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{16}\\sqrt{8}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{4\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."} :step/id #uuid "a7cb447b-1a24-58bb-a8de-9b45b9c2e4bb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416361}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416363 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416357 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "453ed560-2a6a-584b-b775-2ac6a73e45b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416330} {:answer/id #uuid "4e677ef6-4718-55df-b04f-5323f885a475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416332} {:answer/id #uuid "75d24566-becf-5714-8a17-ca7340070ab8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416335} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e29b1a-ee23-527d-a4ce-26194d144439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416340} {:answer/id #uuid "b7f21d52-3d12-5f8b-8383-908db610799b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416343}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416343} :answer-field/id #uuid "9de09353-fb9c-544f-8c67-c4ef2c4abd36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "15ea3713-dc0a-53ca-b70c-c22a6b79a105" :question/math-academy-id "q-86853" :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis for the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c19427b8-35d1-5ec6-a82b-132f5b304d1b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416360}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416364 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10c81834-42ef-5852-b301-a6d3cbb3c8fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416327} {:answer/id #uuid "970d7f5e-257f-5c6c-a87e-441a641de9a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416338} {:answer/id #uuid "9f06a3b6-8e48-5d5d-b47c-2bf33ee89c30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416339} {:answer/id #uuid "fb4520b1-1d35-57bb-b9ad-3e48af9ee8cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416349} {:answer/id #uuid "fd6421c2-2bc0-520a-be99-675b8f795b1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416350}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416327} :answer-field/id #uuid "a54a21cb-93a2-537e-bb2f-71cc7631af9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0f199eb7-3af6-57bb-a681-cef6be591a7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-139476" :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 18\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-1)}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{8}\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."} :step/id #uuid "e5babb95-1720-532d-87a1-a1a7b17c6c2c"}] :activity/title "Projecting Vectors Onto Subspaces in Euclidean Spaces (Arbitrary Bases): Applications" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416325}]}
